 "capsule": "Between 1502 and 1824 the Portuguese Empire (Império Português) built and then began to lose the first global seaborne trading empire, using fortified feitorias and a licensing (cartaz) system imposed from 1502 to dominate Indian Ocean commerce. Viceroys Francisco de Almeida (1505-09) and Afonso de Albuquerque (1509-15) captured Goa (1510) and Malacca (1511), founding the Estado da India, while parallel footholds in Brazil, launched from 1500 and organized as captaincies from the 1530s, grew into a major sugar- and later gold-producing colony. The empire, at its territorial peak around 1820 encompassing roughly 5.5 million square kilometers, fractured when Pedro, son of King John VI, declared Brazilian independence on 7 September 1822, a rupture that reshaped Portugal's remaining dominion by 1824.",
